    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the difference between high-glycemic index foods and low-glycemic index foods, and specifically why the rate of absorption matters for someone managing insulin sensitivity? I'm puting together some notes for a client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-glycemic index (GI) foods, like sugary snacks, are digested and absorbed into the bloodstream very quickly, causing a rapid spike in blood sugar. In contrast, low-GI foods, such as whole grains and most vegetables, are digested much more slowly, leading to a gradual rise in blood sugar. For someone managing insulin sensitivity, this difference is crucial because rapid blood sugar spikes force the pancreas to release a large surge of insulin, which can strain the system over time. Consuming low-GI foods helps maintain steadier energy levels and reduces the demand on insulin production.",
      "source": "target",
