    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful, thanks. One more thing—can you explain how we calculate the Glycemic Load (GL) versus just the GI? I want to make sure I can explain the difference to my client so they don't get overwhelmed by just looking at the index alone.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Glycemic Index (GI) measures how quickly a specific *amount* of carbohydrate raises blood sugar, but it doesn't account for the actual *amount* of carbs you eat. Glycemic Load (GL), however, takes both the GI *and* the carbohydrate content of a typical serving into account. Think of it this way: a small serving of a high-GI food might have a low GL, while a large serving of a low-GI food might still have a moderate GL. GL gives a more practical picture of what a real meal will do to your blood sugar.",
      "source": "target",
